<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP permitted for the DEBUG build's local targets, and for LAN addresses generally.

  ⚠ Required, not a shortcut. Since API 28 the platform default blocks cleartext, and a self-hosted
  Loomarr on a home LAN is almost always plain http:// with no certificate — there is no public DNS
  name to issue one for. Without this, OkHttp fails with a generic exception that reads as "server
  unreachable", which is exactly how it presented on the first emulator run.

  ⚠ `<domain>` matches a HOSTNAME or its suffix — it is NOT a CIDR match. An entry of
  "192.168.0.0" does not cover 192.168.1.47, so listing private ranges that way would look right
  and quietly fail on most real networks. Since a homelab server can be at any RFC1918 address,
  there is no host list that covers them, and `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"` on base-config is
  the only setting that actually works for arbitrary LAN IPs.

  The exposure is bounded deliberately: this permits cleartext to any host the USER configures as
  their own server. It does not weaken TLS where TLS is available — an operator who configures
  https:// still gets a verified connection, and no traffic here is authenticated by anything but
  the device token the user explicitly approved.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
